          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em Frobenius algebra
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            equipped with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structures"} structures
            both of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            and of an a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coalgebra"} coalgebra
            in a compatible way, where the compatibility is different from (more “topological” than) that in a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bialgebra"} bialgebra
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
          %p
            The
            %em Frobenius property
            on an algebra/coalgebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            states that all ways of using
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            product operations and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            coproduct operations to map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mrow
                          %mi m
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^{\otimes^{n+1}} \to A^{\otimes^{m+1}}
            are equal.
          %p
            More generally, Frobenius algebras can be defined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
            to any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            (and even in any
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polycategory"} polycategory
            = succeed "." do
              %em Frobenius monoids
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctor"} endo
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
            is a
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+monad"} Frobenius monad
          %p
            After their original introduction in pure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            in the 1930s, Frobenius algebras later attracted much attention for the role they play in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theory"} topological quantum field theory
                Frobenius algebras encode the structure of
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-dimensional+TQFTs"} 2-dimensional TQFTs
              %p
                Here the underlying vector space of the Frobenius algebra is identified (cf.
                = succeed ")" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+field+theory"} functorial field theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
                over a connected 1-manifold (a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
                for the “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+string"}> closed string
                ” states or a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+interval"} closed interval
                for the “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+string"}> open string
                ” states), the product and coproduct operations are the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
                on a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trinion"} trinion
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
                (“
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair+of+pants"}> pair of pants
                ”) read in either direction, and the Frobenius property reflects the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphisms"} diffeomorphisms
                between
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordisms"} cobordisms
                obtained by gluing
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trinions"} trinions
                along their boundary components.
              %p
                Beware that this is similar to but subtly different from the “non-compact”
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+TQFTs"} 2d TQFTs
                commonly known as the “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+string"}> topological string
                ” (the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
                and the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
                of
                = succeed "-fame)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mirror+symmetry"} mirror symmetry
                %em not
                described by Frobenius algebras (because here one ex-cludes the “cap” cobordism and thus the
                = succeed "-operation" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+objects"} Calabi-Yau objects
                %em
                  %a{:href => "TCFT#Definition"} here
                at
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
              %p
                On the other hand, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRS-theorem"} FRS-theorem
                shows that all
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+2d+conformal+field+theories"} rational 2d conformal field theories
                – hence the compact-target-space sectors of the
                %em physical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
                (such as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+models"} WZW models
                and
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gepner+models"} Gepner models
                %em are
                described by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+monoids"} Frobenius monoids
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
                to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+categories"} modular tensor categories
                (MTCs): Here the ambient MTC encodes the local non-topological (“chiral”) component of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
                (its spaces of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+blocks"} conformal blocks
                = succeed "\u201D." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sewing+constraints"} sewing constraints
          %p Later
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
                (such as now in the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZX-calculus"} ZX-calculus
                = succeed "-process," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunction+collapse"} wavefunction collapse
                (see
                %a{:href => "quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories#MeasurementReferencesQuantumInformationTheoryViaStringDiagrams"} here
                at
                = succeed ")." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories"} quantum information theory via dagger-compact categories
              %p
                One way to understand how this comes about (not the original way, though) is to observe that in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
                which is relevant for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reader+monad"} reader monad
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreader+comonad"} coreader comonad
                for a given
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possible+worlds"} possible
                measurement outcomes merge to a single
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+monad"} Frobenius monad
                (the
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+reader+monad"} quantum reader monad
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/writer+monad"} writer monad
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cowriter+comonad"} cowriter comonad
                for a canonical Frobenius algebra structure on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+span"} linear span
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          %p
            Curiously, these two different roles that Frobenius algebras play in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            are not closely related, in fact they seem somewhat orthogonal to each other.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p There are a number of equivalent definitions of the concept of Frobenius algebra.
          %p
            The original definition is an associative algebra with a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+form"} linear form
            on it.
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#AsAssociativeAlgebraWithLinearForm"}> As an associative algebra with linear form
              \.
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+TQFT"} 2d TQFT
            what crucially matters is that this is equivalent to an associative algebra structure with a compatible coalgebra structure
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#AlgebraCoalgebra"}> As an associative algebra with compatible coalgebra structure
              \.
          %p There are
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#FurtherDefinition"} Further equivalent definitions
          %h3#AlgebraCoalgebra As associative algebra with coalgebra structure
          %p
          #FrobeniusAlgebra.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em Frobenius algebra
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} in
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝟙
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{C}, \otimes, \mathbb{1})
              (for instance
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
              with the usual
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} tensor product of vector spaces
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p
                      (
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"}> unit
                      )
                      = succeed "," do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mi η
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mi 𝟙
                              %mo →
                              %mi A
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta \,\colon\, \mathbb{1} \to A
                  %li
                    %p
                      (
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit"}> counit
                      )
                      = succeed "," do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mi ϵ
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mi A
                              %mo →
                              %mi 𝟙
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \,\colon\, A \to \mathbb{1}
                  %li
                    %p
                      (
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplication"}> multiplication
                      )
                      = succeed "." do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mi μ
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mi A
                              %mo ⊗
                              %mi A
                              %mo →
                              %mi A
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu \,\colon\, A \otimes A \to A
                  %li
                    %p
                      (
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comultiplication"}> comultiplication
                      )
                      = succeed "," do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mi δ
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mi A
                              %mo →
                              %mi A
                              %mo ⊗
                              %mi A
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta \,\colon\, A \to A \otimes A
            %p such that:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi μ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi η
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, \mu, \eta)
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                  (an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
                  when
                  = succeed ")," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mo =
                          %mi Vect
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C} = Vect
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi δ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, \delta, \epsilon)
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonoid"} comonoid
                  (a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coassociative+coalgebra"} coassociative coalgebra
                  when
                  = succeed ")," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mo =
                          %mi Vect
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C} = Vect
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %strong Frobenius laws
                  hold:
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi id
                            %mi A
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi μ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∘
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi δ
                          %mo ⊗
                          %msub
                            %mi id
                            %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi δ
                          %mo ∘
                          %mi μ
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi μ
                          %mo ⊗
                          %msub
                            %mi id
                            %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∘
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi id
                            %mi A
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi δ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (id_A \otimes \mu) \circ (\delta \otimes id_A) = \delta \circ \mu = (\mu \otimes id_A) \circ (id_A \otimes \delta)
            %p
          %p
            In terms of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FrobeniusAlgebra"}
            says:
          %p
            %img{:alt => "string diagrams for the Frobenius algebra axioms", :src => "/nlab/files/frobenius_algebra.jpg"}/
          %p
            The first line here shows the associative law and left/right unit laws for a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonoid"} comonoid
          %p
            In fact, although this seems rarely to be remarked, the two Frobenius laws can be replaced by the single axiom
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi δ
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi μ
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1 \otimes \mu) \circ (\delta \otimes 1) = (\mu \otimes 1) \circ (1 \otimes \delta)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi δ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi δ
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1 \otimes \mu) \circ (\delta \otimes 1) = \delta \circ \mu
            (taken from
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#PastroStreet2008"} Pastro-Street 2008
          %p
            %img{:alt => "Proof that one Frobenius axiom implies both the usual ones", :src => "/nlab/files/frobenius_axioms_one_to_two.png"}/
          %p Here the first and fifth steps use the counitality property of the comonoid structure, the second and fourth steps use the assumed axiom (once in each direction), and the third step uses coassociativity.
          %h3#AsAssociativeAlgebraWithLinearForm As associative algebra with linear form
          %p
            Frobenius algebras were originally formulated in the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            with the following equivalent definition:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong Frobenius algebra
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitality"} unital
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, \mu, \eta)
              equipped with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+form"} linear form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \colon A \rightarrow k
              – to becalled a
              %strong Frobenius form
              – such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \circ \mu
              is a non-degenerate
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ↦
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    u
                    \\;\mapsto\;
                    \\big(
                    v \mapsto \epsilon \circ \mu(v \otimes u)
                    \\big)
            %p
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              with its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+vector+space"} dual space
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi V
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^*
          %p
            From this definition it is easy to see that every Frobenius algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            is necessarily
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional
          %h3#FurtherDefinitions Further definitions
          %p
            There are about a dozen equivalent definitions of a Frobenius algebra.
            %a{:href => "#Street2004"} Ross Street (2004)
            lists most of them.
          %h2#types_of_frobenius_algebras Types of Frobenius algebras
          %h3#commutative_frobenius_algebras Commutative Frobenius algebras
          %p
            We can define ‘commutative’ Frobenius algebras in any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            %strong commutative
            if its associated monoid is commutative — or equivalently, if its associated comonoid is cocommutative.
          %h3#symmetric_frobenius_algebras Symmetric Frobenius algebras
          %p
            We can define ‘commutative’ or ‘symmetric’ Frobenius algebras in any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is
            %strong symmetric
            if
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mi μ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \mu \circ S_{A,A} = \epsilon \mu \,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{A,A} : A \otimes A \to A \otimes A
            is the symmetry, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon\mu
            is the nondegenerate pairing induced as above from the multiplication and the counit. Any commutative Frobenius algebra is symmetric, but not conversely: for example the algebra of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ×
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \times n
            matrices with entries in a field, with its usual trace as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \gt 1
          %p
            A theorem of
            %a{:href => "#Eilenberg1955"} Eilenberg &amp; Nakayama (1955)
            says that in the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            can be equipped with the structure of a symmetric Frobenius algebra if (but not only if) it is
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separable+algebra"} separable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+extension"} extending
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi k
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes_k K
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+algebra"} semisimple
            algebra over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %h3#special_frobenius_algebras Special Frobenius algebras
          %p
          #SpecialFrobeniusAlgebras.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
            %p
              The term “special Frobenius algebra” is not used consistently in the literature, but the main point is to require that the product is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+inverse"} left inverse
              to the coproduct, possibly up to a non-vanishing element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi β
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_A
              in the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
            #eq:ProdLeftInverseToCoprodUpToScaling.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi prod
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi coprod
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi β
                      %mi A
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    prod \circ coprod \,=\, \beta_A \cdot id
            %p
              This condition removes the
              = succeed "-contribution" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus+of+a+surface"} genus
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#NormalFormAndSpiderTheorem"} below
            %p
              But then it is natural to also require the counit to be left inverse to the unit, up to (another) non-vanishing ground field element
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi β
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_1
            #eq:CounitLeftInverseToUnitUpTOScaling.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi counit
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi unit
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi β
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    counit \circ unit \,=\, \beta_1 \cdot id
            %p which also removes the disconnected “vaccum”-contribution, up to scaling.
            %p
              The two conditions
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ProdLeftInverseToCoprodUpToScaling"} (1)
              and
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:CounitLeftInverseToUnitUpTOScaling"} (2)
              on a Frobenius algebra are taken to be the definition of “special Frobenius” in
              = succeed ":" do
                %a{:href => "#FRS-theorem+on+rational+2d+CFT#FuchsRunkelSchweigert02"} Fuchs, Runkel &amp; Schweigert 2002, Def. 3.4 (i)
            %div{:style => "margin: -20px 10px 20px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/FRS-SpecialFrobeniusDiagram.jpg", :width => "400px"}/
            %p However, other authors take the condition for “special Frobenius” to be the single constraint that
            #eq:CoprodLetfInverseToProd.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi prod
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi coprod
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    prod \circ coprod \,=\, id
            %p
              (eg.
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories#CoeckePavlović08"} Coecke &amp; Pavlović 2008, p. 17
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1202.6380"} Fauser 2012, Def. 3.7
            %p
              and may instead call
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ProdLeftInverseToCoprodUpToScaling"} (1)
              the condition to be
              = succeed "." do
                %em trivially connected
            %p Beware that both of these conventions are adhered to by no small number of authors.
          %p
            In the category of vector spaces, any element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            of an associative unital algebra gives a left multiplication map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi L
                            %mi a
                          %mo :
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi b
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↦
                        %mtd
                          %mi a
                          %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  L_a : &amp;A &amp;\to&amp; A \\
                  \&amp;b &amp;\mapsto&amp; a b
                  }
          %p
            which in turn gives a bilinear pairing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: A \times A \to k
            defined by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi tr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(a,b) = tr(L_a L_b)
          %p
            One can show that the algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            can be equipped with the structure of a special Frobenius algebra if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            is nondegenerate, i.e., if there is an isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to A^*
            given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \mapsto g(a, -)
          %p
            In this case, there is just one way to make
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            into a special Frobenius algebra, namely by taking the counit to be
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi tr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon(a) = tr(L_a)
          %p (In any Frobenius algebra, the unit, multiplication and counit determine the comultiplication.)
          %p In fact, all the results of the previous paragraph generalize to Frobenius algebras in any symmetric monoidal category, since the proofs can be done using string diagrams.
          %p
            An associative unital algebra for which the bilinear pairing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            is nondegenerate is called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong strongly separable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separable+algebra"} separable algebra
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Aguiar2000"} Aguiar (2000)
          %p
            To get a feeling for some of the concepts we are discussing, an example is helpful. The group algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[G]
            of a finite group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is always separable but strongly separable if and only if the order of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is invertible in the field
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[G]
            can always be made into a symmetric Frobenius algebra, but only into a special Frobenius algebra when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |G|
            is invertible in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            To see this, we can check that the group algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[G]
            becomes a symmetric Frobenius algebra if we define the counit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo :
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon: k[G] \to k
            to pick out the coefficient of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \in G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo :
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi G
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi g
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi g
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon : \sum_{g \in G} a_g \, g \mapsto a_1 \,.
          %p
            But when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |G|
            is invertible in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[G]
            becomes a
            %em special
            symmetric Frobenius algebra if we normalize the counit as follows:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo :
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi G
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi g
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi g
                  %mo ↦
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon : \sum_{g \in G} a_g \, g \mapsto \frac{a_1}{|G|} \, .
          %p
            We should warn the reader that
            %a{:href => "#Rosebrugh2005"} Rosebrugh et al (2005)
            call a special Frobenius algebra ‘separable’. This usage conflicts with the standard definition of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separable+algebra"} separable algebra
            in the category of vector spaces over a field, so we suggest avoiding it.
          %h3#frobenius_algebras †-Frobenius algebras
          %p
            If a Frobenius algebra lives in a monoidal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%80%A0-category"} †-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi δ
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo †
                  %mo =
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\delta)^\dagger = \mu
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo †
                    %mo =
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\epsilon)^\dagger = \eta
            = succeed "." do
              %strong †-Frobenius algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFTs"} TQFTs
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics+in+terms+of+dagger-compact+categories"} quantum theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} FHilb
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{FHilb}
            can be classified by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H-star-algebra"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mi \
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^\*
                \-algebras
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p
          #VectorSpaceWithLinearBasis.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %br/
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
