          %h3#NormalFormAndSpiderTheorem Normal form and “Spider theorem”
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (normal form for 2d cobordisms)
              %br/
              Every
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topological+space"} connected
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
              with
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi in
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_{in} \gt 0
                  ingoing boundary components
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi out
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_{out} \gt 0
                  outgoing boundary components
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus+of+a+surface"} genus
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            %p
              is equivalent (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"}> diffeomorphic
              ) to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
              of
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi in
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_{in}-1
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trinions"} trinions
                  regarded as
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ×
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 1
                        %mo →
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1 \times S^1 \to S^1
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
                  2-punctured
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tori"} tori
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 1
                        %mo →
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1 \to S^1
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi out
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_{out}-1
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trinions"} trinions
                  regared as
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 1
                          %mo →
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ×
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1 \to S^1 \times S^1
            %div{:style => "margin: -20px 10px 20px 10px"}
              %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
                %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Abrams-2dCobordismNormalForm.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
                %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                  (from
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "#Abrams96"} Abrams 1996, Fig. 3
            %p as shown in the following example for
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi in
                        %mo =
                        %mn 5
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_{in} = 5
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo =
                        %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g = 4
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi out
                        %mo =
                        %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_{out} = 4
            %div{:style => "margin: -20px 10px 20px 10px"}
              %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
                %img{:src => "/nlab/files/2dCObordismNormalForm.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
                %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                  (from
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "#Kock2004"} Kock 2004, p. 64
            %p
          This is discussed in
          = succeed ";" do
            %a{:href => "#Abrams96"} Abrams 1996, Prop. 12
          = succeed "." do
            %a{:href => "#Kock2004"} Kock 2004, §1.4.16
          %p
            By the
            %a{:href => "#RelationTo2DTQFT"} relation
            between 2d cobordism and Frobenius algebras this means equivalently that:
          %p
          #NormalFormForFrobeniusMaps.num_cor
            %h6 Corollary
            %p
              %strong (normal form for Frobenius maps)
              %br/
              Given a Frobenius algebra
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi unit
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi counit
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi prod
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi coprod
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\big(
                    A
                    ,\,
                    unit
                    ,\,
                    counit
                    ,\,
                    prod
                    ,\,
                    coprod
                    \\big)
            %p then every linear map of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi n
                              %mi in
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi n
                              %mi out
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A^{\otimes^{n_{in}}}
                    \\longrightarrow
                    A^{\otimes^{n_{out}}}
            %p which is
            %ol
              %li
                %p entirely the composite of the algebra’s structure maps, ie. of the (co)unit and the (co)product,
              %li
                %p
                  %em> connected
                  , in that it does not decompose as a tensor product of such morphisms,
            %p is equal to the composite, in this order, of
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi in
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_{in}-1
                  copies of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi prod
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} prod
              %li
                %p
                  some number (
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
                  ) of copies of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi prod
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi coprod
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} prod \circ coprod
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi out
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_{out}-1
                  copies of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi coprod
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} coprod
            %p
          %p
          #SpiderTheorem.num_cor
            %h6 Corollary
            %p
              %strong (Spider theorem)
              %br/
              If the Frobenius algebra in Cor.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NormalFormForFrobeniusMaps"}
              is
              %em special
              in that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi prod
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi coprod
                    %mo =
                    %mi id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} prod \circ coprod = id
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:CoprodLetfInverseToProd"} (3)
              then every linear map of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi n
                              %mi in
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi n
                              %mi out
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A^{\otimes^{n_{in}}}
                    \\longrightarrow
                    A^{\otimes^{n_{out}}}
            %p which is
            %ol
              %li
                %p entirely the composite of the algebra’s structure maps, ie. of the (co)unit and the (co)product,
              %li
                %p
                  %em connected
                  in that it does not decompose as a tensor product of such morphisms,
            %p is equal to the composite, in this order, of
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi in
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_{in}-1
                  copies of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi prod
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} prod
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi out
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_{out}-1
                  copies of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi coprod
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} coprod
            %p
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/FrobNormalFormAsSpiderRule.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                From
                %a{:href => "quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories#CoeckeDuncan11"} Cocke  Duncan 2011
          %p
            The “spider theorem” (Cor.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpiderTheorem"}
            %a{:href => "quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories#CoeckeDuncan08"} Coecke &amp; Duncan 2008, Thm. 1
            (it appears unnamed also in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories#CoeckePaquette08"} Coecke &amp; Paquette 2008, p. 6
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZX-calculus"} ZX-calculus
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            (where the Frobenius algebras that appear are those that realize the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+reader+monad"} quantum reader monad
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+monad"} Frobenius
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/writer+monad"} writer monad
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cowriter+comonad"} cowriter comonad
          %h3#frobenius_algebras_in_polycategories Frobenius algebras in polycategories
          %p
            In fact, Frobenius algebras can be defined in any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polycategory"} polycategory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linearly+distributive+category"} linearly distributive category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu:A\otimes A \to A
            but
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⅋
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta :A \to A \parr A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⅋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \parr
            in a linearly distributive category (or between their “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategory"}> multicategorical
            ” analogues in a polycategory) is precisely what is required to write down the composites involved in the Frobenius laws. For instance, we can have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi A
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi δ
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⅋
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi lin
                      %mo −
                      %mi distrib
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⅋
                  %mi A
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo ⅋
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi A
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⅋
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A\otimes A
                  \\xrightarrow{1_A \otimes \delta}
                  A \otimes (A \parr A)
                  \\xrightarrow{lin-distrib}
                  (A \otimes A) \parr A
                  \\xrightarrow{\mu \parr 1_A}
                  A\parr A
          %p and one of the Frobenius laws says that this composite is equal to
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi μ
                  %mi A
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi δ
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⅋
                  %mi A
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes A \xrightarrow{\mu} A \xrightarrow{\delta} A\parr A.
          %p
            This is analogous to how a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimonoid"} bimonoid
            can be defined in any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duoidal+category"} duoidal category
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microcosm+principle"} microcosm principle
            %a{:href => "#Egger2010"} (Egger2010)
            that Frobenius monoids in the linearly distributive category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sup"} Sup
            are precisely
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+category"} *-autonomous
            cocomplete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
            (and hence, in particular, linearly distributive).
          %p
            In polycategorical language we can give another
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unbiased"} unbiased
            definition of a commutative Frobenius monoid: it is equipped with exactly one morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi …
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⏞
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi …
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⏞
                    %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overset{n}{\overbrace{(A,A,\dots,A)}} \to \overset{m}{\overbrace{(A,A,\dots,A)}}
            of each possible (two-sided) arity, such that any (symmetric) polycategorical composite of two such morphisms is equal to another such. The monoid structure consists of the morphisms of arity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2,1)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1,2)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1,0)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2,2)
            are equal. (The morphism of arity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,0)
            is the composite
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon\eta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ()\to ()
            to compare it to.) In other words, the free symmetric polycategory containing a commutative Frobenius monoid is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal
            symmetric polycategory. In this way Frobenius algebras are to polycategories in the same way that monoids are to multicategories.
